const lessons=[
{id:'scan',title:'See before you receive',category:'Awareness',video:['scan','wall'],why:'Your next action starts before the ball arrives. A scan gives information about pressure, teammates and usable space.',cues:['Look while the ball is away, then track it as it arrives.','Find something useful: pressure, space or a teammate.','Update the picture when players move.'],mistake:'Turning your head without identifying anything, or looking away as the ball reaches your foot.',nine:'With fewer teammates, identify one clear next pass and one safe return option. Look beyond the nearest player.',eleven:'Check both nearby pressure and the next line of players. A second scan can reveal a midfielder closing from behind.',solo:'Place two gates near a wall. Pass, glance toward one gate, then receive into it. Alternate. This rehearses the action; it cannot reproduce an unpredictable defender.',duo:'The server passes and moves to close one gate at walking speed. Receiver identifies the open gate and takes the ball there. Swap after five passes.',pressure:'Add a third player as a passive defender, then increase pressure gradually. Make the scan change the first touch.',scenario:'A pass is coming to you. You saw space behind you three seconds ago, but a midfielder has since moved toward you. What information do you need now?',answer:'Refresh the picture before receiving. If pressure has arrived, protect or return the ball instead of turning into it.',evidence:'Describe one opponent or option you saw before receiving, and how it affected your choice.',prereq:[]},
{id:'touch',title:'First touch into the next action',category:'Ball skills',video:['receive','wall'],why:'A useful first touch makes the next pass, dribble or shot possible. Its direction and distance depend on the pressure.',cues:['Open your body when it helps you see and play.','Keep the ball within a playable step under pressure.','Move the touch away from the defender, not blindly forward.'],mistake:'Stopping every ball underneath you, or taking a big touch before checking the space.',nine:'Practice receiving across the body to connect wide and central teammates. Small adjustments can open a passing lane.',eleven:'Consider which line you want to connect to and whether a defender can intercept your next touch.',solo:'From 4–6 yards, pass against a safe wall and receive through a left or right gate. Alternate feet. Count controlled first touches out of ten.',duo:'Pass to each other through a central lane. Receiver takes a first touch into a side gate, then returns the pass. Vary the service gently.',pressure:'Add a defender approaching one side. Select the touch from the pressure rather than using a fixed pattern.',scenario:'The space ahead looks open, but your defender is close on that side. Where should your first touch go?',answer:'Toward space you can actually protect or use. A sideways or backward touch may preserve the next action better than a forced forward touch.',evidence:'Find a receive that immediately allowed a pass or dribble without an extra rescue touch.',prereq:['scan']},
{id:'pass',title:'Pass with purpose',category:'Ball skills',video:['wall','combine'],why:'A good pass reaches a teammate in a way that supports their next action. Direction, weight and timing matter together.',cues:['Look at the target before contact.','Plant beside the ball and use a firm ankle.','Choose the receiving foot or space deliberately.'],mistake:'Passing toward a teammate without noticing the defender or whether the teammate is moving.',nine:'Use short passes to connect triangles. Make the pass comfortable enough for the next player to lift their eyes.',eleven:'Choose between a supporting pass and one that breaks a line. Do not force the longer pass just because the field is bigger.',solo:'Choose a one-yard target on a safe wall. Make ten controlled passes with each foot. Start close; increase distance only when accurate.',duo:'Pass between two gates. Alternate receiving to feet with moving toward a gate for a pass into space. Say which you want.',pressure:'With a third person, play 2v1 in a generous area. Move to keep a passing lane available; rotate the defender.',scenario:'Your teammate is moving forward, but you pass behind them. Why does that slow the attack?',answer:'They must stop or turn to recover the ball. If the passing lane is safe, a pass into their path can preserve forward movement.',evidence:'Identify a pass that helped a teammate play the next action rather than merely reach the ball.',prereq:['scan','touch']},
{id:'turn',title:'Turn, protect, or set it back',category:'Ball skills',video:['receive','shield'],why:'Receiving with your back to goal creates a choice. Turning is useful only when the space and pressure allow it.',cues:['Check behind before the pass.','Use your body to protect the ball.','A return pass can help the team go forward next.'],mistake:'Trying to turn every time, even when a defender is tight behind you.',nine:'A central receiver may connect several parts of the team. Protect the ball long enough for support to arrive.',eleven:'Between the lines, pressure may arrive from both midfield and defense. Be ready for a one-touch set to a supporting player.',solo:'Receive from a wall. Alternate an inside turn, an outside turn, and a return pass. Use cones to mark imaginary pressure.',duo:'Have your brother serve and approach lightly from one side. Turn away if there is room; otherwise protect and return. No pushing.',pressure:'With three players, use a server, receiver and defender. Receiver chooses turn or set based on actual pressure.',scenario:'You receive centrally with a defender tight behind and a teammate facing forward nearby. What could help the attack?',answer:'Protect and set the ball to the forward-facing teammate, then move again. Keeping possession can create the next opening.',evidence:'Explain why you turned or returned a pass in one real moment.',prereq:['scan','touch']},
{id:'dribble',title:'Win a 1v1 with a simple move',category:'Ball skills',video:['feint','touches'],why:'A dribble is valuable when it creates space, advances play or draws a defender to release a teammate.',cues:['Approach under control.','Use a body feint to shift the defender.','Accelerate into the space after the move.'],mistake:'Doing several moves without changing direction or speed, or dribbling into a second defender.',nine:'Recognize open space and try positive 1v1s while keeping an option to pass.',eleven:'Check the covering defender before committing. A successful first move can still lead into crowded space.',solo:'Use one cone as a defender and two exit gates. Feint one way, take the ball the other, accelerate three steps and walk back.',duo:'Play short 1v1 attempts to two gates. Start with light defensive pressure and swap roles after each attempt.',pressure:'Add a supporting player for a 2v1 so the attacker must choose pass or dribble.',scenario:'You beat the first defender but see a second one stepping toward you while your teammate becomes free. What next?',answer:'Release the ball if the passing lane is safe. Drawing the second defender can be the purpose of the dribble.',evidence:'Notice whether your move created a useful pass, shot or space—not only whether you got past someone.',prereq:['touch']},
{id:'shield',title:'Protect possession under contact',category:'Ball skills',video:['shield'],why:'Shielding gives you time to find support or escape. It is a balance and positioning skill, not a contest to hit harder.',cues:['Keep your body between opponent and ball.','Keep knees soft and the ball on the far foot.','Feel pressure, then move toward the available exit.'],mistake:'Standing upright, trapping the ball under both feet, or using a push or grab.',nine:'Use short protection moments to find a teammate. Avoid staying still when a safe pass is available.',eleven:'Anticipate a second opponent arriving. Protect with an exit or supporting pass in mind.',solo:'With a cone behind you, receive, turn sideways and move the ball with the far foot. Practice escaping to both sides.',duo:'In a four-yard square, protect for 10 seconds against light, matched pressure. Rest, swap and repeat. Stop if size or contact makes it unsafe.',pressure:'Add two exit gates. Score by escaping with control, not by holding the ball indefinitely.',scenario:'The defender moves around your left side while the right side is open. What should you consider?',answer:'Move or turn into the available space on the right, or pass if support is safer. Do not remain fixed just to withstand contact.',evidence:'Identify a moment you kept possession and found an exit under pressure.',prereq:['touch']},
{id:'support',title:'Pass, move, and create an angle',category:'Team play',video:['combine','move'],why:'Your job continues after the pass. A new angle gives the teammate on the ball a usable option.',cues:['Move out of a defender’s shadow.','Offer an angle, not the same straight line.','Recognize a give-and-go when the space behind is open.'],mistake:'Watching your pass or running directly behind a defender where you cannot receive.',nine:'Build triangles with nearby players. Keep support close enough for controlled passes.',eleven:'Offer support at different depths so one pass does not isolate the receiver between lines.',solo:'Pass to a wall, move two steps sideways and receive again. Alternate sides and avoid chasing an inaccurate pass at full speed.',duo:'Pass to your brother, move around a cone defender and receive in your path. Swap roles and directions.',pressure:'Add a third player defending the lane. The pair must decide whether to combine or keep possession.',scenario:'Your teammate has the ball but a defender stands between you. What small movement might help?',answer:'Move sideways or change depth to open a passing lane. Calling for the ball without changing the angle does not remove the defender.',evidence:'Find a pass after which your movement gave the receiver another option.',prereq:['pass']},
{id:'runs',title:'Time a run behind the defense',category:'Attacking',video:['behind','wide'],why:'A well-timed run creates a passing window. Running early or straight into a defender can close that window.',cues:['Check the passer’s ability to play forward.','Start where you can see the ball and defensive line.','Curve or delay the run when useful.'],mistake:'Running before the passer can look up, or standing behind the defense and waiting.',nine:'Coordinate with the nearest passer and other forward. Avoid both attackers making the same run.',eleven:'Read the back line, the covering defender and the space between fullback and center back.',solo:'Mark a defender line with cones. Walk through checking short, delaying, then curving behind. Imagine the ball being played at the right moment.',duo:'Your brother looks up before passing as the cue. Time a curved run to receive in space. Swap; discuss the offside line in a real match.',pressure:'Add a defender controlling the line at low speed. Focus on timing before increasing pace.',scenario:'You can run into open space now, but the passer is facing backward under pressure. Should you automatically sprint behind?',answer:'Consider offering support or delaying. The passer may need help to turn or release the ball before the run becomes useful.',evidence:'Identify one run timed to the passer, even if you did not receive the ball.',prereq:['scan','support']},
{id:'cross',title:'Look up and choose the final ball',category:'Attacking',video:['cutback','wide'],why:'A delivery should find a teammate or useful space. A cutback, square pass or reset may be better than an automatic cross.',cues:['Look up before your final touch.','Find the runner, not just the goal area.','Control the pace and direction of the delivery.'],mistake:'Crossing without looking or driving every ball across the goal regardless of teammates.',nine:'Use grounded deliveries and clear target areas to connect with arriving players.',eleven:'Notice near, central and far-side runners plus covering defenders before choosing the target.',solo:'Dribble around a cone in a wide area and pass backward diagonally through a target gate. Alternate sides; use controlled pace.',duo:'One player dribbles wide while the other arrives at a target gate. Deliver into the runner’s path; swap roles.',pressure:'Add a passive defender blocking one target so the wide player must choose a different pass or reset.',scenario:'You reach a wide attacking position, but nobody is arriving in the box. What is a useful alternative to crossing?',answer:'Keep the ball, combine with support or reset the attack. Delivering into an empty area usually gives possession away.',evidence:'Explain the target you selected for one delivery and what you saw before playing it.',prereq:['pass','scan']},
{id:'finish',title:'Create space for an accurate finish',category:'Attacking',video:['finishing'],why:'Finishing starts with the touch that creates a shot and the decision about where and when to strike.',cues:['Take a touch that leaves the ball within striking distance.','Look for the available target.','Balance and accuracy come before maximum power.'],mistake:'Shooting while off balance or taking a first touch so far away that the opportunity disappears.',nine:'Practice controlled shots from useful distances and learn when a teammate has a better angle.',eleven:'Notice pressure, the goalkeeper and a covering defender before choosing an early shot or another action.',solo:'Use a safe goal or two target gates with a clear area behind. Roll the ball, take one setup touch and finish with controlled force. Alternate feet.',duo:'Your brother passes from the side. Receive into a shooting angle and aim at a marked target. Retrieve before the next attempt; no one stands in the shot path.',pressure:'With a coach or extra player, add a recovering defender at light pressure. Keep attempts short and spaced apart.',scenario:'Your first touch leaves the ball behind you and a defender is closing. Must you force the shot?',answer:'No. Recover balance, protect or pass if those options are safer. The original shooting chance may have changed.',evidence:'Identify a touch that created a clear shot, regardless of whether it scored.',prereq:['touch','scan']},
{id:'defend',title:'Delay and guide an attacker',category:'Defending',video:['defend'],why:'Good 1v1 defending protects dangerous space and gives teammates time to recover. Winning the ball is not always the first action.',cues:['Close space, then slow down under control.','Stay balanced and avoid an early lunge.','Guide play according to your team’s plan and available cover.'],mistake:'Sprinting all the way into the attacker or reaching when the ball is still protected.',nine:'Protect the route toward goal and communicate with the nearest teammate.',eleven:'Read cover and the defensive line before deciding how tightly to engage. Do not isolate yourself from support.',solo:'Mark a gate as the goal. Practice an angled approach to a cone, slow down and hold a balanced position. This is footwork rehearsal only.',duo:'Your brother dribbles at walking or jogging pace toward two gates. Stay balanced and guide the route. Swap after short attempts; no sliding.',pressure:'Increase pace only when controlled. Add a second defender to practice the relationship with cover.',scenario:'The attacker is running toward you quickly with space behind. What is the danger of lunging immediately?',answer:'Missing the ball can leave the attacker a clear route through. Slow their progress and protect space while waiting for a better opportunity.',evidence:'Find a moment you delayed an attack or forced a less dangerous action.',prereq:[]},
{id:'cover',title:'Pressure, cover, and stay connected',category:'Defending',video:['cover'],why:'When one teammate pressures, others protect the next spaces and passing lanes. Team defending is coordinated movement.',cues:['Recognize who is nearest to the ball.','Cover behind and inside when appropriate.','Shift together as the ball moves.'],mistake:'Two defenders charging at the same ball while leaving a central passing lane open.',nine:'With fewer defenders, stepping out creates a large responsibility for the remaining players. Communicate who presses and who covers.',eleven:'Connect the back line with midfield. Far-side players protect central space while tracking threats.',solo:'Use three cones for defenders and a ball. Move the ball to different locations and walk through where pressure and cover would be. This is a tactical rehearsal.',duo:'One brother points to a ball-side cone and takes the pressing position; the other moves into a covering angle. Switch roles and explain the gap you protect.',pressure:'With four players, play 2v2 at controlled pace. Pause to inspect whether both defenders are chasing the same opponent.',scenario:'Your teammate steps out to the ball. Should you stand level beside them or protect the space behind?',answer:'Usually provide a supporting angle and depth so you can help if they are beaten, while respecting runners and the team’s defensive plan.',evidence:'Describe one space or passing lane you protected while a teammate applied pressure.',prereq:['defend','scan']},
{id:'transition',title:'React when possession changes',category:'Team play',video:['transition'],why:'Turnovers change everyone’s job immediately. The useful response depends on pressure, support and dangerous space.',cues:['After winning it, check forward and supporting options.','After losing it, assess whether pressure is possible.','If you cannot help pressure, recover to protect danger.'],mistake:'Stopping to react emotionally or chasing the ball when the team needs recovery and cover.',nine:'Every player may need to switch roles quickly. Find a simple forward or supporting pass after a regain.',eleven:'Coordinate the first pressure with teammates; recover if the press cannot be supported. Follow the coach’s triggers.',solo:'Dribble toward a gate, then use a timer cue to turn toward a home cone and reset. Movement practice cannot teach the full decision without opponents.',duo:'Play short 1v1 rounds between opposite gates. On a turnover, immediately switch roles. Stop and explain the first decision after a few rounds.',pressure:'Use 2v2 or 3v3 with immediate changes of direction on a regain. Keep short rounds and recovery breaks.',scenario:'You lose the ball and the opponent has already passed beyond you. What may help more than chasing from behind?',answer:'Recover toward a useful defensive position, protect a lane or track a runner. Immediate pressure is useful only when you can influence the play.',evidence:'Identify your first useful action after one regain and one loss.',prereq:['scan','defend']},
{id:'switch',title:'Use width and change the point of attack',category:'Team play',video:['switching','wide'],why:'When opponents crowd one side, space may open elsewhere. Support behind the ball can make the switch possible.',cues:['Keep an option behind the ball.','Look across the field before forcing play.','Move into the new space as the ball travels.'],mistake:'Forcing a long pass through pressure or everyone following the ball to the same side.',nine:'A switch may be two simple passes through a central teammate. Keep useful width without becoming unreachable.',eleven:'Connect fullback, central players and the far side. Larger distances make the timing and weight of passes important.',solo:'Set two gates at different angles to a wall. Receive from one direction and pass toward the other, imagining a central supporting role.',duo:'Use a triangle of cones. One player passes to the central player, who receives across the body and passes through the far gate. Rotate roles.',pressure:'With a third player, let a defender close one gate. Find the open side or keep the ball if neither is available.',scenario:'Three opponents crowd your wing while a teammate is free on the far side. Must you attempt the long pass yourself?',answer:'No. A safe backward or central pass may create the angle for a teammate to switch play.',evidence:'Spot a moment when a supporting pass helped the team reach the other side.',prereq:['pass','support','scan']},
{id:'gkbuild',title:'Goalkeeper: support and distribute',category:'Goalkeeping',video:['gkbuild'],why:'The goalkeeper can give the team an extra passing option. Safe angles, scanning and distribution help the team keep possession.',cues:['Offer an angle before the teammate needs you.','Scan both sides before receiving.','Reposition after the pass in case possession changes.'],mistake:'Standing hidden behind an opponent or passing into pressure without checking.',nine:'Keep communication simple and help the nearest defenders form clear passing angles.',eleven:'Read the press across a wider defensive line and connect to the safe free player rather than following a fixed pattern.',solo:'Use a wall and two target gates. Receive with the feet, open the body and pass through a chosen gate. Keep it controlled.',duo:'Your brother acts as a defender and passes back. Move to offer an angle, receive and pass to a target gate. Swap roles.',pressure:'With a coach and another player, add gentle pressure and practice deciding between short options and a safe longer release.',scenario:'Your nearest defender is marked but you have another clear option. Should you use the marked player because that was the original plan?',answer:'Update the decision from the pressure. Use a safe available option and communicate; a plan is not a reason to pass into a trap.',evidence:'Identify a time your movement gave a defender a safe return pass.',prereq:['scan','touch','pass']},
{id:'gkposition',title:'Goalkeeper: position and communicate',category:'Goalkeeping',video:['gkposition'],why:'Position relative to the ball, goal and teammates helps the goalkeeper prepare for what comes next.',cues:['Adjust as the ball moves.','Be balanced and ready before a possible shot.','Use clear, early information for teammates.'],mistake:'Watching the ball from one fixed spot or calling only after the danger has arrived.',nine:'Practice tracking the ball across a smaller field and keeping defenders informed about nearby opponents.',eleven:'Reassess distance from goal as play moves upfield and watch for space behind the defensive line.',solo:'Mark a small goal with cones. Walk between ball-location markers and identify a balanced position relative to the ball and goal. No diving.',duo:'Your brother walks the ball across three locations. Adjust position, set your feet and communicate. Use no shots or collisions for this rehearsal.',pressure:'Practice real shot situations only with appropriate goalkeeper instruction and a suitable surface. This library is not a solo diving program.',scenario:'The ball travels from a central position to a wide attacker. Can the goalkeeper remain in the same place?',answer:'The angle has changed. Adjust relative to the ball and goal, then become balanced before a possible delivery or shot.',evidence:'Explain one positioning adjustment or early instruction that helped the defense.',prereq:['scan']}
];
const positionPaths={
 winger:{name:'Winger / wide midfielder',short:'W',path:['scan','touch','dribble','support','runs','cross','transition','defend'],mission:'Create or use width, threaten space behind and make the final action useful.',have:'Check whether to stay wide, receive to feet or run behind. Combine when a defender has help.',lose:'React to the nearby ball and recover into your team’s defensive shape.',win:'Offer a forward outlet if available; otherwise help keep the first pass.',defend:'Track the appropriate wide threat and protect the inside route according to the coach’s plan.',nine:'In a 3-3-2, the wide midfielder often connects attack and defense. In a 3-2-3, a winger starts higher but still has recovery responsibilities.',eleven:'Coordinate with a fullback, a central midfielder and the striker. Avoid occupying the same lane and depth as the overlapping teammate.'},
 striker:{name:'Striker / center forward',short:'ST',path:['scan','touch','turn','shield','runs','finish','support','transition'],mission:'Create scoring windows and connect the team’s forward play.',have:'Alternate showing to feet and threatening behind. Find a shooting angle or set a teammate.',lose:'Follow the agreed first-pressure direction; avoid a solo chase that opens the center.',win:'Offer a forward target or a secure set-back option.',defend:'Help guide the opponent’s build-up toward your team’s pressure.',nine:'With two forwards, coordinate one checking short while the other threatens behind. With one forward, stay connected to support.',eleven:'Read both center backs and supporting midfielders. Your movement can create space for a winger or attacking midfielder.'},
 am:{name:'Attacking midfielder',short:'AM',path:['scan','touch','turn','pass','support','runs','finish','transition'],mission:'Connect midfield to attack and create the next opening.',have:'Find space between opponents, scan behind and receive with a useful next action.',lose:'React centrally and protect or pressure the immediate next pass with support.',win:'Find the forward-facing option and support the attack.',defend:'Stay connected to midfield and help screen central passes.',nine:'A separate number 10 may not exist. A central midfielder or second forward can perform parts of this role.',eleven:'In a 4-2-3-1, work between the midfield and defensive lines, combining with the striker and wide players.'},
 cm:{name:'Central midfielder',short:'CM',path:['scan','touch','pass','support','turn','switch','cover','transition'],mission:'Connect teammates, offer support and help control the center.',have:'Create a passing angle and know where pressure is before receiving.',lose:'Protect central space while assessing who can pressure.',win:'Help secure the first pass and recognize a forward opportunity.',defend:'Stay connected to nearby midfielders and track runners through your area.',nine:'Central players often combine defending, build-up and attacking jobs. Avoid leaving the middle empty together.',eleven:'Coordinate with a holding midfielder and other central players. Stagger positions so teammates have different passing options.'},
 dm:{name:'Holding midfielder',short:'DM',path:['scan','touch','pass','support','switch','cover','defend','transition'],mission:'Provide a safe connection in possession and protect the space ahead of the defense.',have:'Offer angles to defenders and connect forward when the lane is safe.',lose:'Protect the central route and recognize runners beyond you.',win:'Secure the ball or connect forward without forcing a risky turn.',defend:'Screen dangerous passes and communicate with center backs.',nine:'The holding role may be shared by a pair or the central player in a three. Talk about who stays when the other goes.',eleven:'As a single 6 or part of a pair, balance support with protection behind the attack. Do not follow every ball into a wide area.'},
 fb:{name:'Fullback / outside defender',short:'FB',path:['scan','touch','pass','defend','cover','support','cross','transition'],mission:'Protect wide space and support the attack at the right time.',have:'Offer width or a supporting angle. Advance when team cover and the coach’s plan allow it.',lose:'Recover to protect the route toward goal and reconnect with defenders.',win:'Provide a safe outlet and look for the wide or central teammate.',defend:'Control the wide 1v1 and check runners near the far post when play is opposite.',nine:'In a back three, the outside defender often has less cover to leave behind. In a back four, fullback responsibilities become more distinct.',eleven:'Coordinate with winger, center back and holding midfielder. An overlap should complement their positions rather than leave an unprotected lane.'},
 cb:{name:'Center back',short:'CB',path:['scan','pass','touch','defend','cover','switch','transition','support'],mission:'Protect dangerous central space and start controlled attacks.',have:'Offer a clear angle to the goalkeeper and choose a safe progressive or supporting pass.',lose:'Check the ball, nearest runner and space behind the line.',win:'Secure possession when possible and help teammates spread into options.',defend:'Coordinate stepping out, covering and tracking runners. Communicate early.',nine:'One central defender in a back three must coordinate both outside defenders. Do not all step toward the same ball.',eleven:'Work as a central pair or part of a three. One player engaging often needs another protecting depth.'},
 gk:{name:'Goalkeeper',short:'GK',path:['scan','gkposition','touch','pass','gkbuild','transition'],mission:'Protect the goal, organize teammates and support possession.',have:'Offer an angle and distribute to a safe option.',lose:'Adjust position relative to goal, ball and space behind defenders.',win:'Assess whether an early release is available or the team needs time to organize.',defend:'Move with the ball, prepare for the next action and communicate early.',nine:'Build confidence with clear positions and short distribution. Use goalkeeper-specific instruction for handling and saves.',eleven:'Read the wider field and defensive line while contributing to build-up. Technical saving work still needs a goalkeeper coach.'}
};
